it's a crime that is notoriously  difficult to prosecute last year just  1.7 percent of reported rapes led to a  criminal charge the lowest number in a  decade rape is an underreported crime is  estimated that only about 15% of people  who experience sexual violence actually  end up going to the police so we wanted  to investigate how the system is  treating people who do decide to report  and whether they get in the support that  they need so at the end of last year I  was raped in my bedroom by somebody that  I knew that I had previously met before  somebody that I trusted and then you  made the decision to go to the police  yes I reported it basically hours after  it happened and since reporting it I've  just felt a massive lack of support that  day was like the hardest day because I  spent ten hours in a room with male  police officers couldn't see my family  couldn't see any friends no one was  allowed to even know where I was and  since then I just don't feel that I've  been given any support or any help it's  just unbelievable really when I mean  even reporting it must be a very  difficult decision to make  yeah I thought I was doing the right  thing and I thought because I had  reported it hours after I thought that  that's putting me in the best position  that's what you're told to report as  soon as possible I'm keen to kind of  talk about a couple of the issues that  you've experienced and first thing you  had to hand your phone over yeah so I  didn't hand it over straight away  because they took the perpetrators phone  and I assumed that would be enough I  wasn't really sure why they had to take  mine but then a few months later they  did ask for mine and yet they've had it  this entire year to be honest I didn't  mind giving it up because I would do  anything for my case basically it's more  that they've had it for so long and I  should have been given monthly updates  and I wasn't I had to chase the police a  lot to find out what's happening it's  more that they haven't dealt with it  like they've now decided not to even use  it so I'm confused why they even took it  in the first place another actually  experiences around counseling and just  explained cos I think my viewers to be  quite alarmed I guess to know what the  situation is with accessing counseling  before trial yeah so you can have  pretrial therapy is what it's called and  is literally described as a limited  counseling  in the definition of it you can't talk  about the one reason that you need the  counseling so you're not allowed to talk  about anything that is in your police  notes which is obviously what happened  to you  and that's because it can all be used as  evidence the defense can request your  notes then some parts of what you said  can be used against you or the therapist  can be seen to be guiding you over what  happened or what to say if it does go to  court how important do you think  counseling is how much would it benefit  you to be able to just talk freely I  think it massively would have helped me  I think so many people credit counseling  and therapy as being life-changing as it  being this amazing thing where it can  really really help you and really be  beneficial to you and it's really  frustrating for me that I felt like I  desperately needed it and I haven't been  able to have that and then the third  issue that has been a really big issue  for you it's been housing hasn't it so  because it happened in my bedroom again  this is because I reported it you'll  then not allow back there because it  becomes a crime scene so until the  police go to your room or your flat or  wherever it happened and take everything  they need you are not allowed back there  and then obviously when you are allowed  back there it then becomes a very  difficult place to be and to live so I  went to the council for help and I was  told that if you're fleeing sexual  violence or domestic abuse they do not  have to help you so I was told that from  their point of view they could argue you  have intentionally made yourself  homeless it was so upsetting because I  think a lot of the time we think that  survivors aren't believed and I don't  think that that's the issue  the issue isn't that you're not believed  is that you're not heard is that you're  reaching out for help and it's the same  with mental health as well people reach  out for help and they're trying to get  help and the fact is the help isn't  there and it's not being given so where  have you been living so I was put in a  hostel after a while of waiting I was  put in a hostel and it was a hostel with  ninety percent men did you feel safe no  I didn't the floor on the hostel that  was women only constantly have male  residents on and I constantly had to  write written complaints to get myself  out of there and to get myself moved  somewhere appropriate I had to fight to  be taken seriously that that place  wasn't suitable for me what's your  message to  any politician who might be listening to  this interview know what needs to happen  I think that we need to actually listen  to what survivors are saying that they  want and that they shouldn't be deciding  what's best for us open up the  conversation if we're saying that we  want counseling then I feel we should be  able to have that and that there  shouldn't be an argument against that  especially from you know people who  haven't been through it and haven't  experienced it  Sarah Greene the co-director at the end  violence against women coalition says  that this is not an isolated case  so women's organizations around the  country are seeing increasingly worrying  justice outcomes so we're seeing a big  fall in the numbers of cases that  actually get charged there might be a  bit of an investigation and then no  charge we are sure that the bar has been  raised on charging cases so a huge surge  like an almost tripling in the numbers  of people reporting rate to the police  and that is recent rapes as well as  rapes that may have happened a long time  ago and then at the same time we've got  this fall-off in the numbers of cases  that actually get charged there's a very  low chance now frankly that if you  report a rape you make the initial  police call or visit you will ever end  up in court it's something like one in  25 of those reports are ending up in  court only a few years ago that was one  in five and you know we need to be  looking at this this is urgent the  government is holding a review into how  raping serious sexual offences are  handled by the justice system and  pressure is mounting from politicians  such as Labor's Shami Chakrabarti for  them to act rape cases are particularly  difficult and therefore particularly  sensitive to a lack of resources and  indeed to demoralize professionals I  have no doubt I have no doubt that  people are being put off coming forward  this is just a national embarrassment it  seems that there is some guidance that  is still in place but dating back to the  early 2000s that is discouraging people  from getting therapy pretrial on the  spurious basis that somehow this will  compromise their credibility as we  is that it will be fair game to argue as  it they're not telling the truth they've  somehow been coached or given a false  memory by a therapist and this is their  by discouraging people from getting a  vital therapy that they need and at a  time when the country is getting to  grips just a little more with mental  health problems after a series of rape  trials collapsed last year it's perhaps  understandable that there's been a  renewed focus or making sure that cases  have a realistic chance of prosecution  but speaking to survivors it's hard not  to conclude that victims are being  failed by the system and the support  that they need just isn't there in a  parliament consumed by brexit and the  theatres of a leadership contest there  is a risk that other issues are being  put on ice now in response to our report  the Crown Prosecution Service sent us a  statement there has been no change of  policy in how the CPS makes charging  decisions in rape cases these offences  can have a devastating impact will  always seek to prosecute where there is  sufficient evidence to do so there are  strict rules which mean that digital  data should only be accessed when it  forms a reasonable line of inquiry and  private information not connected to the  case must not be shared with the defense  all showing courts furthermore witnesses  and complainants should not be  discouraged or prevented from seeking  therapy and counseling before or during  the trial process

